          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I can't breathe properly and my inhaler isn't working. My chest feels really tight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** Trouble breathing that does not get better with an inhaler, together with chest tightness, is an emergency. Do not wait to see if it passes. If someone is with you, ask them to call for you. Do not drive yourself.\n\n**Why this is urgent:** The emergency guidance says breathlessness that comes on suddenly or happens at rest needs immediate care. It also says an asthma attack that does not improve after reliever treatment is a red flag (source: \"Severe difficulty breathing,\" emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance). Chest tightness with breathlessness is also treated as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise (source: \"Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack,\" emergency medicine and cardiology society guidance). These are different causes, and they cannot be told apart without a clinician. That is why the guidance says to seek help now.\n\n**Get help immediately if you notice any of these:**\n- You can only speak in short phrases, or you have to stop for breath between words.\n- Your lips, tongue, face or fingertips look blue or grey.\n- You feel very drowsy, confused, or unusually agitated.\n- Your throat feels tight, your lips or tongue are swelling, or you have hives or dizziness. These can be signs of a severe allergic reaction (source: \"Anaphylaxis and severe allergic reaction\").\n\n**While you wait** (source: \"Severe difficulty breathing\"):\n- Sit upright. This is usually the easiest position.\n- Loosen any tight clothing.\n- Stay as calm as you can, because anxiety makes breathing harder.\n- If you have a reliever or emergency medicine prescribed to you, use it as your own action plan says.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "emergency",
